  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Friday...\n\nUpper level ridging will dominate the forecast through much of \nnext week. Hot to extremely hot temperatures are forecast each \nday, with today being the hottest day of the forecast period. Look\nfor highs in the upper 90s to near 110F today, and 90s to 100sF \nmost other days. Extreme Heat Warnings remain in effect this \nthrough 9 PM MDT Sunday for most lower elevations of southern \nMontana and Sheridan County, Wyoming. For the foothills west and \nsouth of Billings, Heat Advisories remain in effect until 9 PM MDT\nSunday. There will be daily chances for isolated shower and \nthunderstorm activity, with the primary impacts being brief heavy \nrain thanks to elevated PWAT values (around to slightly above an \ninch through Monday, then around 0.75\" the rest of the week) and \nerratic wind gusts (some could be strong, especially today).\n\nThere is a Marginal to Slight Risk (2/5) for isolated/scattered \nstrong to severe thunderstorms this afternoon and evening across \nthe area. The main risk is strong to severe wind gusts, in excess\nof 60 mph. The strongest wind gusts could reach 75 mph or higher.\nWith a wave moving through this afternoon, storms are expected to\ndevelop over the mountains and move from southwest to northeast. \nSoundings paint an interesting picture, with deep inverted-V \nprofiles with high clouds building in by mid- afternoon. As \nmentioned before, PWATs will be above normal and the risk of brief\nheavy rainfall cannot be ruled out despite very low relative \nhumidity values across the lower elevations and foothills in the \n10 to 20 percent range. The risk for the strongest winds is \nevident in the latest HiRes model runs, which depict a band of \nthunderstorms firing off of the high terrain west and south of \nBillings by mid-late afternoon. The magnitude of the wind gusts \nshown amongst these models ranges from 50 to 75 mph, with the \nhighest gusts shown by the HRRR, NAM, and RRFS amongst others. \nThere has generally been consensus that the stronger gusts will \ntrack into northeast Montana though, with even higher gusts \npossible there. Another concern with storms will be lightning.\n\nThe combination of extremely hot temperatures, very low relative \nhumidity values, and mostly dry thunderstorms with strong to \nsevere winds and lightning will create Critical Fire weather \nconditions today. As a result, a Red Flag Warning is in effect for\nsouthern Montana, portions of northern Wyoming and western South \nDakota from Noon MDT to Midnight tonight. Please take care not to \ncause a spark.\n\nRelative humidity values are generally expected to be in the \nteens and low 20s percent through the week. With temperatures \ndrying out fuels, elevated fire weather conditions are expected. \nThe lack of strong winds will be one factor helping to keep lower \nfire risk.\n\nFinally for those keeping up on the July 25th drama. Billings \nairport has never (since 1934) reached 100F on this date. The \ncurrent forecast has a high of 102F for today. The above normal \nPWAT values and afternoon convection could mitigate the extent of \nsurface heating with cloud cover though. The July 25th record is \n98F, so it is possible to break the record and still not reach \n100F.\n\nMatos",
